# What may enter the build context, stated as an allowlist.
#
# The Dockerfile assembles the image with two `COPY . <workdir>` instructions --
# one in the `assets` stage and one in the final stage -- so this file, and
# nothing else, decides what those two immutable layers contain. Both of them
# say so at their own site, and this is the file they name.
#
# It used to hold one line, `**/.git`, which meant every path that happened to
# be on the builder's disk was baked in: `config/local.*` and
# `config/runtime.json` (a live session secret and runtime state),
# `config/development.*` and `config/production.*` deployment config,
# `node_modules`, the 435 MB `public/components` tree, the generated CSS, and any
# workstation residue sitting alongside them.
#
# With the staged build that shape is not merely untidy, it is a build failure,
# and that is measured rather than predicted. The final stage copies the source
# in and then lays the production-only dependency tree over it with
# `COPY --from=deps ... ./node_modules`, which MERGES into whatever is already
# at that path instead of replacing it. A single-stage build was safe from this
# because its `npm ci` deleted `node_modules` before installing; nothing in the
# final stage does that. So a host `node_modules` in the context survives into
# the shipped layer, and the final stage's own audit -- the step that asserts no
# declared devDependency has a directory under `node_modules` -- exits 1 and
# fails the build. The same mechanism is why a host `public/components` would be
# shipped twice, once from the context and once from the `assets` stage, with
# the first copy's bytes still occupying the lower layer.
#
# A deny-list is not sufficient here, and that is a measurement rather than a
# preference: this checkout carries an untracked directory (`blitzy/`) that
# `.gitignore` does not name, so a .dockerignore that merely mirrored
# `.gitignore` would still have copied it in. An allowlist makes the default
# "excluded", so a top-level path nobody has thought about yet needs no new
# rule to stay out.
#
# WHERE THAT DEFAULT REACHES, STATED EXACTLY, BECAUSE THE SCOPE IS EASY TO
# OVERSTATE. Section 1's `*` is a single-segment pattern, so it denies
# TOP-LEVEL entries only. Section 2 then re-admits a tracked directory by name,
# and that re-admits everything beneath it -- including an untracked file
# sitting in there on the builder's disk. So "excluded by default" is a
# guarantee about the top level, and below it the guarantee is only as wide as
# the patterns in section 3.
#
# `config/` is therefore allowlisted FILE BY FILE in section 3 rather than by
# excluded-name, because it is the directory that holds secrets by convention
# and enumerating the bad names only ever covers the names already thought of.
# The source directories (`lib`, `public`, `test`, `docs`, `scripts`, `static`,
# `serverside`) are admitted wholesale: a per-file allowlist over their ~548
# tracked files would go stale on every added file, silently and with nothing
# checking it, which trades a small hygiene exposure for a large correctness
# one. The residual is real and bounded: an UNTRACKED file left inside one of
# those directories is copied into the image. `git status --porcelain` shows
# any such file, and `git archive HEAD | docker build -` builds from committed
# content only when a guaranteed-clean context is wanted.
#
# The two maintenance costs of this shape, written down because they are
# invisible otherwise:
#   1. A NEW TRACKED TOP-LEVEL ENTRY MUST BE RE-ADMITTED IN SECTION 2 BELOW OR
#      IT WILL NOT REACH THE IMAGE. That list is the output of
#      `git ls-files | cut -d/ -f1 | sort -u`, so that command is also how it
#      is checked.
#   2. A NEW TRACKED FILE UNDER `config/` MUST BE RE-ADMITTED IN SECTION 3, for
#      the same reason. That list is `git ls-files config`. A new file under any
#      other admitted directory needs no edit.
#
# Docker matches these patterns against the whole context-relative path with Go
# path matching, where a single `*` does not cross a `/`. Two consequences are
# load-bearing below: a single-segment `*` excludes only top-level entries, so
# re-admitting a directory re-admits everything under it (which `**` would not
# do), and the last pattern that matches a path wins, so the three sections
# have to stay in this order.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Exclude every top-level entry, including dotfiles and untracked residue.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
*

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Re-admit the tracked top-level entries, and only those.
#
# Deliberately the complete tracked set rather than a hand-picked minimum: it
# keeps the image's contents identical to what the previous whole-checkout COPY
# produced apart from the residue this file exists to keep out, which is the
# smallest change that fixes the problem. Pruning tracked source would be a
# separate decision with its own risk.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
!.bowerrc
!.dockerignore
!.gitignore
!.nvmrc
!CHANGELOG.md
!CODE_OF_CONDUCT.md
!COMPONENTS.md
!CONTRIBUTING.md
!Dockerfile
!GETTING_STARTED.md
!LICENSE
!README.md
!app.js
!catalog-info.yaml
!config
!docker-compose.yml
!docs
!lib
!mkdocs.yml
!package-lock.json
!package.json
!public
!scripts
!serverside
!static
!test
!vite.config.mjs

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Re-exclude the ignored, generated and secret-bearing paths that live
#    inside the directories section 2 just re-admitted.
#
# Section 2 pulls whole directories back in, so everything here would otherwise
# return with them. `**/` prefixes reach any depth, matching the unanchored
# `.gitignore` entries they correspond to; paths without one are anchored at the
# context root exactly as their `.gitignore` counterparts are.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Nested repository metadata, at any depth.
**/.git

# Local, runtime and deployment configuration -- the secret-bearing half of this
# file's purpose, and the one directory admitted file by file.
#
# `config/*` denies every direct child, then the tracked files are re-admitted
# by name below. That is deliberately not the same thing as listing the files to
# KEEP OUT, which is what this block used to do: an excluded-name list covers
# `config/local.yaml` and `config/runtime.json` because someone thought of them,
# and covers `config/customer-acme-secrets.yaml` not at all. Denying the
# directory and naming what may enter inverts that, so an unanticipated file
# dropped in here -- by an operator, a tool, or a half-finished deployment --
# cannot reach a shipped layer, and no rule has to be added to keep it out.
#
# This also subsumes what the excluded-name list did: `config/*` matches
# `config/local.{yaml,yml,json}`, `config/runtime.json`,
# `config/runtime.json.tmp-*`, `config/development.*`, `config/production.*`,
# `config/migration.json` and `config/migrationDb.json`, none of which is
# re-admitted.
#
# Note the two tracked lookalikes that MUST stay in the context and are
# re-admitted explicitly below: `config/local.example.yaml` and
# `config/production.yaml.dist` are build inputs, and they are exactly the
# names a `config/local.*` or `config/production.*` wildcard would have eaten,
# because `*` happily matches "example.yaml" and "yaml.dist".
#
# The list is `git ls-files config`, all 13 entries, all at depth 1 -- there is
# no subdirectory under config/, so no nested re-admission is needed. A tracked
# file added here, or a subdirectory introduced here, must be added below.
config/*
!config/api_routes.js
!config/app.config.js
!config/aws.js
!config/constants.js
!config/db.js
!config/default.yaml
!config/local.example.yaml
!config/log.js
!config/production.yaml.dist
!config/redis.js
!config/reserved.yaml
!config/routes.js
!config/test.yaml

# Installed dependencies, at any depth: the root install is performed by
# `npm ci` inside the image, and each serverside unit installs its own, so a
# host tree copied in would only shadow them with the wrong platform's binaries
# -- and at the final stage it would survive into the shipped layer and fail
# that stage's devDependency audit, which is the failure described at the top of
# this file.
**/node_modules

# Fetched and generated frontend assets. `scripts/fetch-components.js` publishes
# public/components inside the image and `npm run build:css` writes
# public/css/base.css and public/css/embed.css there, so copies from the host are
# stale by construction -- and shipped twice, because the final stage copies both
# out of the `assets` stage by name.
#
# Note the shapes here, because two of them are one character apart:
# public/css/base.css and public/css/embed.css are the two generated files,
# while public/css/challenge.css and everything under public/css/embed/ are
# tracked assets. The literal pattern `public/css/embed.css` cannot match
# `public/css/embed/embed.css`, which has a `/` where the pattern has a `.`, so
# the tracked assets stay in the context -- verified by listing the context.
public/components
public/vendor
public/_published
public/404.html
public/50x.html
public/maintenance.html
public/js/dist
public/css/base.css
public/css/base.css.map
public/css/embed.css
public/css/embed.css.map
public/css/publish
lib/views/courses/download/course.css
lib/views/courses/download/course.css.map

# Migration backups written next to the migrations they came from.
lib/migrations/backups

# Build and editor residue. The top-level forms are already excluded by section
# 1; these reach the nested copies, matching the unanchored `.gitignore` rules.
**/.sass-cache
**/npm-debug.log
**/build.log
**/test-*.js
**/.DS_Store
**/*.swp
**/*.pyc
**/*.sublime-project
**/*.sublime-workspace
**/Vagrantfile
**/.vagrant
**/.agignore
